WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.672
Module 4: reading patterns honestly.

00:00:02.672 --> 00:00:07.646
Shape before average, stories before
claims, and outliers as clues.

00:00:07.646 --> 00:00:12.768
In about forty minutes you'll learn
what every chart shows and hides,

00:00:12.768 --> 00:00:18.781
how to compare groups safely, and how to
write a pattern in the storytellers' own

00:00:18.781 --> 00:00:19.226
words.

00:00:19.733 --> 00:00:23.291
Open the survey's analytics
page and work down it.

00:00:23.291 --> 00:00:25.069
Summary cards at the top.

00:00:25.069 --> 00:00:26.990
A trend of stories per day.

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:30.903
Then the filter bar, which
redraws every chart at once.

00:00:30.903 --> 00:00:35.883
Then a chart for each question: the
triangle, the sliders, the stones,

00:00:35.883 --> 00:00:39.156
the story explorer and
your context questions.

00:00:39.156 --> 00:00:43.140
Below that, a cross-tab to
compare two choice questions,

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.772
and buttons to export every response.

00:00:45.772 --> 00:00:50.610
The page doesn't update itself, so
reload it to pick up new stories.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:55.232
The triad card shows each story
as a dot, an average marker,

00:00:55.232 --> 00:00:58.538
and the average as a
percentage for each corner.

00:00:58.538 --> 00:00:59.984
Three things to know.

00:00:59.984 --> 00:01:03.084
First, the average is
a point, not a pattern.

00:01:03.084 --> 00:01:06.321
It can sit in empty space
between two clusters,

00:01:06.321 --> 00:01:09.695
so check the dots before
quoting the percentages.

00:01:09.695 --> 00:01:12.519
Second, density is smoothed and relative.

00:01:12.519 --> 00:01:17.616
It colours areas from low to high
compared with this chart's busiest spot,

00:01:17.616 --> 00:01:23.194
so "high" in a chart of twenty stories
isn't the same amount as "high" in a chart

00:01:23.194 --> 00:01:24.227
of two hundred.

00:01:24.227 --> 00:01:29.186
Use density to see where stories bunch,
and dots to count and read them.

00:01:29.186 --> 00:01:30.839
Third, count the region.

00:01:30.839 --> 00:01:36.004
"About thirty stories in the deadline
corner" beats "the deadline is high".

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:42.079
A slider card gives you five numbers:
mean, median, standard deviation,

00:01:42.079 --> 00:01:43.650
minimum and maximum.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:45.928
Read them as a warning light.

00:01:45.928 --> 00:01:51.271
When the mean sits well away from
the median, stories lean or split.

00:01:51.271 --> 00:01:57.557
Northwind's "when did you hear" slider
had a mean of seventy-one and a median of

00:01:57.557 --> 00:02:01.250
eighty: most stories
high, a smaller group low.

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:05.650
A large standard deviation,
around thirty on this scale,

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:09.578
means stories are spread
wide, maybe in two camps.

00:02:09.578 --> 00:02:13.192
Then look at the histogram
and name its shape.

00:02:13.192 --> 00:02:16.885
Then click into each hump
and read the stories.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.992
Stones have three views,
and each hides something.

00:02:20.992 --> 00:02:24.009
Averages, which opens first, hides splits.

00:02:24.009 --> 00:02:27.744
Individual shows every
placement, coloured by stone,

00:02:27.744 --> 00:02:30.474
and lets you click through to stories.

00:02:30.474 --> 00:02:35.647
Density shows where placements bunch,
but it pools every stone together,

00:02:35.647 --> 00:02:38.664
so it can't tell you which stone is which.

00:02:38.664 --> 00:02:40.172
Check the legend too.

00:02:40.172 --> 00:02:43.764
Counts differ because people
can leave stones out.

00:02:43.764 --> 00:02:45.991
In Northwind's decisions study,

00:02:45.991 --> 00:02:49.870
"a chance to give input"
averaged absent and middling.

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:55.258
The individual view showed why that
misleads: most placements sat far left,

00:02:55.258 --> 00:02:59.784
and a separate group of thirty-eight
sat present, but hindered.

00:02:59.784 --> 00:03:03.376
People were asked, and felt
it made no difference.

00:03:03.867 --> 00:03:09.975
The story explorer gives you a count, average
words, top words and a keyword search.

00:03:09.975 --> 00:03:12.084
It shows eight stories first.

00:03:12.084 --> 00:03:14.993
Show all opens the list fifty at a time,

00:03:14.993 --> 00:03:18.192
and Load more stories
brings the next fifty.

00:03:18.192 --> 00:03:20.883
The search looks through all of them.

00:03:20.883 --> 00:03:24.737
Northwind's decisions study
had two hundred and four,

00:03:24.737 --> 00:03:27.791
and a search for "huddle" found forty-one.

00:03:27.791 --> 00:03:33.245
But search finds only the word you
type, so try the other words people use.

00:03:33.245 --> 00:03:36.808
The Response Explorer
opens from any dot or card,

00:03:36.808 --> 00:03:39.499
showing the story and its placements,

00:03:39.499 --> 00:03:43.353
and you can step through
stories with the arrow keys.

00:03:43.353 --> 00:03:47.861
To read everything, use the
Responses page or export the data.

00:03:47.861 --> 00:03:50.261
And treat top words as a pointer,

00:03:50.261 --> 00:03:53.970
not a meaning: they tell
you which stories to read.

00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:57.142
Filters are your main analysis tool.

00:03:57.142 --> 00:04:00.932
Slice by a context answer,
like clinic or hospital.

00:04:00.932 --> 00:04:02.939
Or slice by a slider range.

00:04:02.939 --> 00:04:06.358
Filter "when did you
hear" to its top quarter,

00:04:06.358 --> 00:04:10.222
and every other chart shows
only stories heard late.

00:04:10.222 --> 00:04:15.722
In Northwind's data, those stories
average forty-one on "worked around it,

00:04:15.722 --> 00:04:16.985
or made it work".

00:04:16.985 --> 00:04:20.255
The stories heard early
average seventy-two.

00:04:20.255 --> 00:04:24.491
Before you open a slice, check
its size in the cross-tab,

00:04:24.491 --> 00:04:27.613
which rolls groups under ten into "Other".

00:04:27.613 --> 00:04:31.849
The filter bar hides any group
of fewer than ten stories,

00:04:31.849 --> 00:04:36.828
and you hold the same line in every
export, slide and conversation.

00:04:36.828 --> 00:04:42.922
And remember, two answers that go together
in the same stories are a relationship,

00:04:42.922 --> 00:04:44.334
not proof of cause.

00:04:44.833 --> 00:04:49.684
Here's Northwind's first study
again, sliced by where people work.

00:04:49.684 --> 00:04:55.783
On the slider about how people hear news,
clinic stories lean toward word of mouth,

00:04:55.783 --> 00:04:57.767
with a mean of sixty-eight.

00:04:57.767 --> 00:05:03.206
Hospital stories lean toward official
announcements, with a mean of forty.

00:05:03.206 --> 00:05:08.717
But site doesn't explain everything:
twelve clinic stories still sit at the

00:05:08.717 --> 00:05:09.673
official end.

00:05:09.673 --> 00:05:14.302
And the support stone splits
cleanly, absent in clinic stories,

00:05:14.302 --> 00:05:16.140
present in hospital ones.

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:21.431
Judge four statements: supported, not
supported, or needs more evidence.

00:05:21.933 --> 00:05:27.465
Two hundred stories is too many to read
once and remember, so work in passes.

00:05:27.465 --> 00:05:29.404
Survey every chart's shape.

00:05:29.404 --> 00:05:32.421
List two or three surprises, as questions.

00:05:32.421 --> 00:05:32.853
Slice.

00:05:32.853 --> 00:05:36.875
Then read systematically:
every story in a small region,

00:05:36.875 --> 00:05:41.904
and a spread of at least ten to
fifteen in a big one, from the middle,

00:05:41.904 --> 00:05:44.131
the edges and different groups.

00:05:44.131 --> 00:05:47.148
Read across the boundary of a cluster too,

00:05:47.148 --> 00:05:52.177
because stories just inside and just
outside show what separates them.

00:05:52.177 --> 00:05:55.984
Check whether each story's
text agrees with its dots.

00:05:55.984 --> 00:05:59.504
Then name the pattern in
the storytellers' words.

00:05:59.504 --> 00:06:01.515
Keep a simple log as you go.

00:06:01.515 --> 00:06:03.311
It's your evidence later.

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:07.285
Experts go looking for the
stories that don't fit.

00:06:07.285 --> 00:06:08.819
There are three kinds.

00:06:08.819 --> 00:06:14.534
Rare positions: only fourteen of Northwind's
two hundred and four stories sat near

00:06:14.534 --> 00:06:16.486
"the people doing the work".

00:06:16.486 --> 00:06:22.272
One describes a manager trying two rotas
with the team, then choosing one together.

00:06:22.272 --> 00:06:27.918
Stories whose text and dots disagree: a
welcome decision, still heard about late.

00:06:27.918 --> 00:06:32.309
That tells you the problem is
process, not unpopular decisions.

00:06:32.309 --> 00:06:36.282
And different voices, like a
manager who also heard late.

00:06:36.282 --> 00:06:41.231
Use outliers as clues and bright
spots, never as findings on their own.

00:06:41.231 --> 00:06:44.995
And take extra care: outliers
are easier to recognize.

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:50.856
You met the first trap in the Mosaic
course: two camps hiding behind one mean.

00:06:50.856 --> 00:06:52.504
Here are the other four.

00:06:52.504 --> 00:06:56.144
An average sitting in empty
space, where no story is.

00:06:56.144 --> 00:07:00.127
An average that looks honest
but hides a separate cluster,

00:07:00.127 --> 00:07:03.355
like the thirty stories
in the deadline corner.

00:07:03.355 --> 00:07:07.132
Stone density that blends
every stone into one surface.

00:07:07.132 --> 00:07:12.625
And a tiny group's average, like eleven
managers, which swings with every story.

00:07:12.625 --> 00:07:16.608
And one trap in words: never
turn a position into a score.

00:07:16.608 --> 00:07:19.767
"Sixty-one percent heard
late" is wrong twice.

00:07:19.767 --> 00:07:23.613
It's stories, not staff, and
a slider isn't a yes or no.

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:27.248
You know the Finding Card
from the Mosaic course.

00:07:27.248 --> 00:07:30.139
The Pattern Card is its
Mosaic Signal cousin,

00:07:30.139 --> 00:07:34.765
with six fields: the pattern in the
storytellers' terms, where it shows,

00:07:34.765 --> 00:07:39.134
how many stories out of the total,
two or three paraphrased stories,

00:07:39.134 --> 00:07:41.896
your confidence and
why, and what it isn't.

00:07:41.896 --> 00:07:46.522
That last field rules out the tempting
overclaim, like calling "willing,

00:07:46.522 --> 00:07:47.871
not able" resistance.

00:07:48.367 --> 00:07:49.083
Your turn.

00:07:49.083 --> 00:07:54.818
Using the sliced wave 1 data, write two
Pattern Cards: one on how news of change

00:07:54.818 --> 00:07:57.327
reaches people, and one on support.

00:07:57.833 --> 00:08:01.073
Now the module check: five
questions, four to pass.

00:08:01.567 --> 00:08:07.159
Here's your Analysis Guide: every view,
what it hides, and the reading method.

00:08:07.159 --> 00:08:08.019
Download it.

00:08:08.533 --> 00:08:09.211
To recap.

00:08:09.211 --> 00:08:14.556
Every view hides something: averages
hide splits, density pools stones,

00:08:14.556 --> 00:08:17.567
and search finds only the word you type.

00:08:17.567 --> 00:08:20.954
Read the slider's numbers
as a warning light.

00:08:20.954 --> 00:08:26.600
Slice by context and by slider range,
and under ten stories, don't show it.

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:27.729
Read in passes.

00:08:27.729 --> 00:08:29.536
Treat outliers as clues.

00:08:29.536 --> 00:08:33.525
And write Pattern Cards with
counts out of the total.

00:08:33.525 --> 00:08:36.537
Next, waves, Culture Dig and presenting.
